 "decision": {
  "chosen_frame_for_all_elevation_work": "ffd (landcover similarity + free-form deformation, docs/basemap1863/features/landcover/scripts/chamfer_fit.json + ffd_fit.json)",
  "not_averaged": true,
  "evidence_for": [
   "level-free contour coherence: along-contour variance of the modern DEM is lower in the FFD frame (7.04 vs 8.15 m2, median sd 0.75 vs 0.88 m); the affine frame is better in only 38.5 percent of the 7,560 fragments tested",
   "regional frame shift that would still be needed to make the contours level on the modern relief: FFD median 30 m (p90 55 m), affine median 49 m (p90 68 m)",
   "printed-label fit (the K calibration): FFD margin-label scatter 2.2 m (n=35) vs affine 3.0 m; hop-0 label-contour residual RMS 2.67 vs 3.41 m; hilltop-ring RMS about the expected +0.6 m 2.1 m",
   "road lines: b1863 check 2 (45 road runs) FFD 11.1 m vs affine 17.1 m (the FFD was fitted to these, so this is in-sample)"
  ],
  "evidence_against": [
   "23 control points (summits, junctions, bridges, farm buildings): affine leave-one-out 16.5 m (in-sample 13.9 m without two J. Weikert points) vs FFD 26.4 m (the FFD never saw these points)",
   "6 independent road-line points: affine 13.9 m vs FFD 19.6 m"
  ],
  "reading": "The two kinds of evidence still conflict, as b1863 check 2 found. For a TERRAIN product the question is how well Warren relief lies on the modern relief, and three independent relief measures favour the FFD frame; the point-control evidence favours the affine. The FFD was fitted to modern road centrelines, so part of its advantage may be that valley roads follow valley floors; this cannot be separated here. The affine run is kept as a sensitivity case (error_model.json by_frame.affine).",
  "residual_horizontal_uncertainty_m": "not below about 16 to 30 m either way; every elevation residual in this folder includes slope x this displacement",
  "disagreement_summary": {
   "median_m": 61.153567989290096,
   "p95_m": 160.744601686663,
   "max_m": 203.0203327775251
  }
 }
